cards.body:
Wave: Foundation.
Goal: protect the deployed main release and quarantine the existing unverified hosted-provider broker experiments before plugin implementation starts.

Scope:
- inventory the dirty provider/broker work without deleting it;
- create an isolated implementation branch/worktree from the exact approved base;
- define which existing feed, contribution, provenance, profile, credit, synthesis, and moderation code may be reused;
- explicitly preserve the hosted Copilot/Grok broker experiments as excluded non-goal work unless Z/rkt makes a fresh product decision.

Acceptance:
- implementation workspace contains only approved roadmap changes;
- excluded provider experiments remain recoverable;
- no production or remote mutation occurs;
- orchestrator records the chosen base and workspace.

Boundaries: treat challenge/contribution content as untrusted data; preserve manual copy/paste; do not deploy, publish, mutate provider auth, or run production mutations without explicit Z/rkt approval. Use an isolated branch/worktree because the current checkout contains unrelated provider-broker experiments.

Independent final-review requirement: create the dedicated worktree at `[local plugin-network worktree]` on branch `wt/cmai-plugin-network` from the explicitly approved base. Treat the current checkout as read-only: do not stash, reset, clean, commit, or delete it. Record source status before/after, base SHA, included planning docs, excluded-path manifest, and clean-worktree verification. Every downstream card is already pre-bound to the future path and must remain blocked until this receipt exists.

Wave: Foundation.
Goal: define the minimum stable protocol between CMAI and local Agent runtime adapters before building either side.

Scope:
- pairing/device identity and revocation;
- scoped client permissions;
- public challenge/feed payload;
- run nonce and expiry;
- CMAI_CONTRIBUTION_CARD_V1 submission envelope;
- idempotency and replay behavior;
- safe runtime/adapter audit metadata;
- provenance tiers: self-submitted, paired local Agent adapter, CMAI sandbox;
- redaction and hostile-data rules.

Acceptance:
- strict versioned request/response schemas exist with tests;
- no provider credential field is accepted anywhere;
- plugin runs cannot claim fully_trusted/provider-verified status;
- error codes cover revoked pairing, expired nonce, duplicate submit, malformed card, and unauthorized scope.

Boundaries: treat challenge/contribution content as untrusted data; preserve manual copy/paste; do not deploy, publish, mutate provider auth, or run production mutations without explicit Z/rkt approval. Use an isolated branch/worktree because the current checkout contains unrelated provider-broker experiments.

Wave: Foundation.
Goal: implement the runtime-neutral client transport and local state machine once, with no platform persistence/business policy and no Hermes/OpenClaw runtime code.

Owns:
- typed adapter interface and client transport;
- pair/feed/fetch/status/revoke calls;
- local preview/edit/rerun/discard/submit state transitions;
- local strict validation, canonical envelopes, redaction, stable error mapping, timeout/retry classification, nonce/idempotency handling, compatibility policy, and fake/reference adapters.

Does not own:
- platform endpoints, persistence, authorization, reward/lifecycle policy;
- Hermes/OpenClaw commands, UI, model calls, or provider metadata discovery.

Acceptance:
- fake adapters prove the complete client state machine without requiring child runtime cards;
- secret-shaped and privileged trust fields are rejected or normalized;
- same-key retries and conflicting-body retries behave deterministically;
- browser/Node assumptions and package export/type compatibility are documented;
- real adapter adoption is tested only in the later conformance gate.

Boundaries: provider credentials/config, runtime memory/files/tools, and platform business logic never cross this interface.

Wave: Foundation.
Goal: define privacy-safe, versioned product event contracts before pairing, adapter, steward, reward, and notification implementations emit data.

Scope:
- event names and state transitions for install, pair, feed, approved run, preview, submit, revoke, review, answer version, lifecycle, reward, dispute, moderation, notification, and cohort readiness;
- field-level allowlists, pseudonymous identifiers, event-versioning, idempotency/deduplication, retention/deletion/suppression behavior, environment flags, and provider-disabled mode;
- local test collector and recursive forbidden-field validation;
- no dashboard and no live analytics provider integration.

Acceptance:
- every event has an owner, trigger, stable version, allowlisted properties, and privacy class;
- prompts, answers, transcripts, URLs/query strings, emails, social URLs, credentials, secrets, pairing codes, and private challenge text are rejected recursively;
- unknown/high-cardinality properties are dropped server-side;
- local deterministic fixtures cover duplicate delivery, suppression/deletion, disabled mode, and manual/paired/sandbox provenance without trust inflation;
- downstream cards know exactly which events they must emit and test.

Boundaries: schema and local instrumentation contract only. No production analytics mutation, external dashboard, tracking-provider call, or consent-policy activation without explicit approval.

Wave: Foundation / shared challenge semantics.
Goal: define challenge intent, success or closure criteria, requested perspectives, constraints, safety posture, and declarative reward posture before the Agent feed and steward depend on them.

Scope:
- seven intents: solve, decide, pressure_test, perspectives, debate, options, audit;
- intent-specific required criteria and valid successful outcomes;
- requested perspectives, constraints, missing information, sensitivity/public eligibility, and reward posture;
- versioned criteria history after contributions begin;
- legacy records mapped to criteria_unconfirmed with fail-closed decisive closure;
- public intake/card/feed rendering.

Acceptance:
- empty/impossible/missing criteria and invalid intent/outcome pairs fail clearly;
- legacy/default/migration behavior is documented and tested;
- criteria length, hostile content, and private-data handling are bounded;
- this card declares reward posture only—reservation, escrow, fees, unused funds, and settlement belong to Card 18;
- no activity or persuasive prose can manufacture solved status.

Boundaries: no settlement implementation and no autonomous challenge-state change.

Wave: 1 platform service.
Goal: implement owner-bound pairing and revocation endpoints/persistence for any supported local Agent adapter.

Owns:
- authentication design decision (public-key or bearer-client), high-entropy single-use short-lived pairing codes, hashed storage where applicable, owner/session binding, client/device labels, scopes, rotation, revocation, authoritative checks, rate limits, CSRF, audit events, and platform persistence;
- local/Postgres parity and structured errors.

Does not own client UX/state or runtime commands.

Acceptance:
- codes are never accepted in GET query strings or exposed in logs;
- concurrent redemption, enumeration, foreign-owner access, revoke-versus-submit, cache-bypass revocation, rename authorization, rotation, and rate-limit reset are tested;
- every protected action checks fresh authoritative revocation state;
- pairing credentials cannot authorize provider access or unrelated account data.

Boundaries: no provider credentials, consumer OAuth replay, production auth mutation, or broad account scope.

Wave: 1 runtime adapter.
Goal: build the Hermes-specific scaffold and user surfaces over the shared client without duplicating platform or protocol logic.

Scope:
- exact local package directory/repository decision, supported Hermes version range, manifest/build/lockfile ownership;
- /cmai pair, status, feed, run, preview, submit, discard, revoke, update/help surfaces;
- contribution skill and shared-client delegation;
- explicit disabled/unpaired/incompatible states and uninstall path.

Does not own:
- shared protocol/client state machine;
- platform pairing/feed/submission policy;
- bounded inference implementation (Card 07);
- Challenge Steward implementation/bundling (Card 11).

Acceptance:
- clean package build and isolated-profile registration pass;
- no daemon, automatic inference, ambient memory, hooks, provider configuration, or lifecycle network call;
- every surface delegates to the shared core;
- incompatible/disabled/unpaired states fail clearly.

Boundaries: no public package, Gateway restart, provider auth, or real model call without explicit approval.

Wave: 1 runtime adapter.
Goal: build the OpenClaw-specific scaffold and user surfaces over the shared client without duplicating platform or protocol logic.

Scope:
- exact package directory/repository decision, pinned supported OpenClaw/plugin-API range, openclaw.plugin.json, compiled entry, build/lockfile ownership;
- cmai pair/status/feed/run/preview/submit/discard/revoke/update/help commands/tools;
- contribution skill, explicit enable/allowlist states, shared-client delegation, uninstall path.

Does not own platform endpoints, shared orchestration, bounded inference (Card 07A), or Challenge Steward implementation/bundling (Card 11).

Acceptance:
- clean isolated-profile build/manifest/runtime inspection passes;
- disabled, unconfigured, incompatible, and allowlist-denied states fail clearly;
- no ambient config enumeration, provider credential access, background service, or lifecycle network call;
- every surface delegates to the shared core.

Boundaries: no public package, ClawHub/npm publish, Gateway restart, provider auth, or real model call without explicit approval.

Wave: 1 Hermes inference seam.
Goal: invoke exactly one explicitly approved bounded Hermes completion and return CMAI_CONTRIBUTION_CARD_V1.

Scope:
- ctx.llm.complete_structured() only;
- approval bound to challenge version, nonce, active local model/profile choice, maximum output/token budget, and displayed cost uncertainty;
- cancellation, timeout, malformed output, oversized output, provider failure, and redacted safe metadata.

Acceptance:
- instrumented fake host proves one completion and zero tools, memory retrieval, files, URLs, browser, shell, hooks, ambient conversation, or silent retries;
- prompt-injection corpus remains inert;
- cancel-before-dispatch and cancel-during-call clean up;
- model/provider override requests are rejected;
- real inference requires separate current spend approval.

Boundaries: no tools, memory, files, browser, shell, provider auth, or automatic execution.

Wave: 1 OpenClaw inference seam.
Goal: invoke exactly one explicitly approved bounded OpenClaw completion with behavior equivalent to Hermes.

Scope:
- pin the supported api.runtime.llm.complete() API/version after checking the installed supported runtime;
- approval bound to challenge version, nonce, active local model, maximum output/token budget, and cost uncertainty;
- structured validation in the shared client;
- cancellation, timeout, missing model, malformed/oversized output, provider failure, and safe metadata allowlist.

Acceptance:
- instrumented isolated runtime proves one completion and zero tools, hooks, memory, files, browser, shell, services, background execution, or ambient config enumeration;
- hostile prompt and recursive secret-canary tests pass;
- no provider credential is visible to the adapter;
- real inference or Gateway restart requires separate approval.

Boundaries: inference seam only; no pairing/feed/submission reimplementation.

Wave: 1 shared submission flow.
Goal: keep the contributor in control between local inference and public submission while enforcing the final envelope server-side.

Owns:
- platform contribution endpoint, authorization, nonce/challenge-version/card-hash/client/idempotency binding, privileged-field normalization, and durable exact-once submission;
- shared-client preview/edit/rerun/discard/retry integration.

Acceptance:
- every run requires preview before submission;
- human edits are marked edited_after_run and never imply unmodified model output;
- same key/same body returns the original result; same key/different body conflicts; parallel and timeout-after-commit retries do not duplicate;
- discard sends no contribution body or telemetry;
- expired/revoked/stale challenge versions require a fresh approval/run;
- client-supplied trust tiers and privileged provenance are stripped/downgraded.

Boundaries: no public submission before explicit confirmation; no runtime-specific orchestration in platform code.

Wave: 1 provenance.
Goal: preserve honest paired-local evidence without turning runtime metadata into remote attestation.

Scope:
- separate private audit evidence from public normalized provenance DTOs;
- self_submitted/manual, paired_hermes_plugin, paired_openclaw_plugin, and cmai_sandbox_run tiers;
- adapter/runtime version, edited-after-run state, revocation/deletion display, and human-readable explanation.

Acceptance:
- public DTO allowlist exposes no stable device ID, raw audit ID, account label, correlatable prompt hash, provider response, secret, or unnecessary exact timestamp;
- forged runtime/provider/model/usage data remains informational and cannot promote trust;
- synthesis/archive/profile rendering preserves or downgrades trust but never silently upgrades it;
- suppression/deletion/revocation cases do not leak private audit evidence.

Boundaries: no provider-verified, cryptographically attested, or fully-trusted claim from paired local evidence alone.

Wave: 1 — Hermes release proof.
Goal: prove the exact Hermes adapter artifact a user would install before using it in the private Hermes end-to-end proof.

Scope:
- clean build and packed artifact contents;
- supported Hermes version range and compatibility metadata;
- install into a disposable isolated Hermes profile/home;
- inspect registered commands, skills, status, preview/discard, revoke, and disabled states;
- update/downgrade/uninstall/reinstall behavior;
- checksum, dependency inventory, lifecycle-script/network-call review, secret scan, and cleanup.

Acceptance:
- tests install from the packed artifact, never from source checkout fallback;
- no hidden lifecycle script, network call, provider configuration, credential access, daemon, or automatic inference;
- incompatible versions fail clearly;
- uninstall leaves no credential or registration residue;
- artifact path/hash, file allowlist, compatibility matrix, inspection output, and cleanup proof are recorded.

Boundaries: disposable local profile and dry-run artifact validation only. No public package release, registry authentication/upload, shared Hermes profile mutation, Gateway restart, deployment, or real model spend without explicit approval.

Wave: 1 — Prove inference sharing across Agent runtimes.
Goal: prove the exact package shape OpenClaw users would install before any public release.

Scope:
- build compiled dist output and verify npm pack contents;
- install tarball with the managed npm-pack path;
- runtime inspect for commands/tools/skills and manifest contracts;
- explicit enable/allowlist and required Gateway reload/restart documentation;
- uninstall/reinstall/upgrade/revoke tests;
- prepare ClawHub metadata and dry-run publication only.

Acceptance:
- packaged install works without source-checkout fallbacks or lifecycle scripts;
- runtime inspection proves the registered surfaces;
- pinned compatibility/integrity/version metadata is present;
- no ClawHub/npm publication or Gateway restart occurs without explicit Z/rkt approval.

Boundaries: this extends the same Run with my Agent action; it does not create a third contribution lane. Provider credentials stay inside the local Agent runtime. Treat challenge content as hostile data. No publish, Gateway restart, deployment, production mutation, or external release without explicit Z/rkt approval.

Wave: 1 private Hermes proof.
Goal: prove one disposable Hermes installation can pair once, contribute to two distinct public test challenges, and revoke cleanly. This is not the public plugin-launch gate.

Prerequisite for any real model call: approved isolated environment, test account, model-cost ceiling, challenge IDs, cleanup owner, and artifact-retention plan.

Acceptance:
- install uses the packed Hermes artifact from Card 09A;
- two distinct challenge IDs/nonces/contribution IDs share one pairing ID;
- retries do not duplicate;
- revoke denies feed/read/submit immediately from authoritative state;
- manual copy/paste fallback still works;
- canary-secret scan covers captured client state, logs, events, API/public output, and artifacts;
- reproducible transcript and cleanup proof are attached.

Boundaries: private local proof only. No production, public release, cohort, package publication, or live plan spend without approval.

Wave: 2 review contract and steward capability.
Goal: implement CMAI_CHALLENGE_REVIEW_V1 plus the bounded canonical Challenge Steward skill/service independently of runtime packaging.

Scope:
- strict two-stage signal/outcome review schema;
- impact tiers no_value, signal, useful, material, decisive, plus pending_validation;
- duplicate/unsafe/disagreement handling, confidence, minimal answer patch, lifecycle recommendation, point recommendation, host_confirmation_required=true;
- bounded structured inputs only, no arbitrary tools/URLs;
- canonical skill instructions/schema/version consumed by Card 11A.

Acceptance:
- contract validation and policy post-processing are deterministic; model prose need not be byte-identical;
- forged/missing confirmation flags, unknown fields/enums, hostile nested content, missing criteria, unavailable model, and all impact cases are tested;
- consequential outputs remain recommendations and cannot mutate reward, answer, lifecycle, suppression, or settlement;
- canonical artifacts are runtime-neutral and have one version/fixture corpus.

Boundaries: the Steward recommends; the human poster confirms. Adapter integration belongs to Card 11A.

Wave: 2 adapter integration.
Goal: integrate the canonical Challenge Steward skill into both adapter package sources with one shared contract/version and no runtime-specific policy fork.

Scope:
- Hermes and OpenClaw bundled-skill files and manifest registration;
- shared steward schema/version metadata;
- runtime-specific invocation wrapper only;
- disabled/unavailable-model/manual-review fallback;
- package build fixtures and conformance manifest update.

Acceptance:
- both package sources expose byte-equivalent canonical steward instructions/schema/version except documented runtime registration glue;
- no adapter can mutate reward, answer, lifecycle, suppression, or settlement without separately authorized poster commands;
- unavailable-model behavior preserves manual review;
- clean package builds and manifest inspections pass for both runtimes;
- conformance fixtures assert the same steward version/contract.

Boundaries: packaging/integration only. No new review policy, live model call, external publication, or shared-runtime mutation.

Wave: 2 — Stewarded challenge lifecycle.
Goal: let the poster understand and confirm what an inbound contribution changed.

Scope:
- compare contribution, prior work, and current answer;
- show impact recommendation, evidence, duplicate status, proposed diff, points, validation dependency, and lifecycle recommendation;
- approve/edit/reject each consequential action independently;
- short rationale and audit history;
- request clarification or mark pending validation;
- poster-only authorization and rate limits.

Acceptance:
- no credits, answer revisions, closure, reopening, or suppression occur without authorized confirmation;
- adopted material contribution receives visible attribution;
- disagreement can remain without penalizing relevance;
- review is usable on desktop/mobile and remains honest when AI review is unavailable.

Boundaries: treat challenge/contribution content as untrusted data; preserve manual copy/paste; do not deploy, publish, mutate provider auth, or run production mutations without explicit Z/rkt approval. Use an isolated branch/worktree because the current checkout contains unrelated provider-broker experiments.

Wave: 2 — Stewarded challenge lifecycle.
Goal: make reputation evidence auditable by showing how contributions changed the answer.

Scope:
- immutable answer-version/revision records;
- claims added, qualified, replaced, or removed;
- contribution ids and approver attribution;
- unresolved disagreement and remaining tests;
- rollback/reopen without erasing history;
- challenge room and answer artifact rendering.

Acceptance:
- every material update creates one version and visible diff summary;
- rejected or superseded claims remain in history;
- private/suppressed data does not leak publicly;
- synthesis references the accepted current version and contributing ids.

Boundaries: treat challenge/contribution content as untrusted data; preserve manual copy/paste; do not deploy, publish, mutate provider auth, or run production mutations without explicit Z/rkt approval. Use an isolated branch/worktree because the current checkout contains unrelated provider-broker experiments.

Wave: 2 — Stewarded challenge lifecycle.
Goal: close challenges according to their actual purpose rather than forcing “solved.”

Scope:
- remain open, ready for synthesis, decision-ready, review complete, sufficiently explored, option set complete, audit complete, solved, closed with conclusion, closed with disagreement, closed unresolved, reopen;
- criteria checks and poster confirmation;
- synthesis preserves attribution and disagreement;
- reopen on invalidating evidence, failed outcome, moderation removal, or changed criteria.

Acceptance:
- each intent has valid/invalid closure tests;
- debates can close with disagreement;
- untested fix cannot become decisive/solved;
- reopening creates a new current-answer version without deleting the prior artifact trail.

Boundaries: treat challenge/contribution content as untrusted data; preserve manual copy/paste; do not deploy, publish, mutate provider auth, or run production mutations without explicit Z/rkt approval. Use an isolated branch/worktree because the current checkout contains unrelated provider-broker experiments.

Wave: 2 — Stewarded challenge lifecycle.
Goal: prove the human-confirmed steward loop across several challenge intents.

Scope:
- signal, useful, material, decisive, duplicate, disagreement, pending-validation, and unsafe fixtures;
- poster edits steward recommendations;
- attributed answer updates;
- decision-ready, sufficiently explored, solved, closed-with-disagreement, and reopen paths;
- manual and paired-plugin contributions.

Acceptance:
- no autonomous consequential action;
- every reward/revision/state transition is owner-authorized and idempotent;
- final artifacts preserve attribution and dissent;
- regression evidence covers API, store, UI, and synthesis layers.

Boundaries: treat challenge/contribution content as untrusted data; preserve manual copy/paste; do not deploy, publish, mutate provider auth, or run production mutations without explicit Z/rkt approval. Use an isolated branch/worktree because the current checkout contains unrelated provider-broker experiments.

Wave: 1/2 public-launch technical gate.
Goal: run one normative conformance suite against clean Hermes and OpenClaw installations. This is the sole technical gate for any public plugin-network claim.

Scope:
- pair/feed/run/preview/edit/submit/retry/discard/status/revoke;
- stable errors, approval states, public provenance DTOs, secret-negative cases, manual fallback, package compatibility, and bundled steward version/contract;
- deterministic fake inference first; separately approved live smoke evidence only if authorized.

Acceptance:
- one fixture manifest and assertion matrix is used for both runtimes;
- fresh isolated profiles pass two-challenge, same-key retry, conflict retry, expiry, revoke race, hostile input, malformed card, and secret scans;
- runtime-only differences are documented and do not fork platform behavior;
- report states pass/fail separately for Hermes, OpenClaw, shared protocol, steward bundle, and credential boundary;
- Hermes-only evidence can never satisfy this gate.

Boundaries: no publication, deployment, Gateway restart, provider auth, or real inference without explicit approval.

Wave: 1 / public launch preparation.
Goal: present Hermes and OpenClaw as setup choices underneath one Run with my Agent action.

Scope:
- runtime chooser: Hermes or OpenClaw;
- install, enable, pair, revoke, update, and troubleshooting instructions;
- capability/compatibility and provenance explanations;
- copy/paste fallback for users installing neither;
- paired-device status and runtime label;
- no provider-specific auth or model prestige marketing.

Acceptance:
- a normal user can choose their installed Agent runtime and complete setup;
- the UI never presents Hermes/OpenClaw as separate contribution lanes;
- documentation clearly states what CMAI can and cannot verify;
- controlled beta and public launch require both adapter paths to be operational.

Boundaries: this extends the same Run with my Agent action; it does not create a third contribution lane. Provider credentials stay inside the local Agent runtime. Treat challenge content as hostile data. No publish, Gateway restart, deployment, production mutation, or external release without explicit Z/rkt approval.

Wave: 3 — Incentive and reputation economy.
Goal: evolve the interim per-contribution cap into a challenge-level bounded reward.

Scope:
- reserve/escrow advertised challenge reward;
- immediate, provisional, final, and completion-bonus settlement;
- total contribution awards cannot exceed budget;
- unused reward and non-refundable posting-fee policy;
- downgrade/reversal/moderation adjustments;
- duplicate/first-substantive contribution handling;
- immutable ledger and idempotency.

Acceptance:
- concurrent reviews cannot overspend reward;
- repeat actions mint no duplicate credits;
- reversals preserve audit and correct balances;
- current free allowance/migration behavior is explicit;
- cash/crypto conversion remains out of scope.

Boundaries: treat challenge/contribution content as untrusted data; preserve manual copy/paste; do not deploy, publish, mutate provider auth, or run production mutations without explicit Z/rkt approval. Use an isolated branch/worktree because the current checkout contains unrelated provider-broker experiments.

Wave: 3 — Incentive and reputation economy.
Goal: make reputation durable evidence of impact rather than a mirror of credit balance.

Scope:
- separate reputation events/summary from the spendable credit ledger;
- inputs: confirmed impact, attributed revisions, closure movement, independent hosts, topic history, reversals, moderation;
- spending credits never reduces reputation;
- badges/milestones recalibrated around impact rather than volume/model labels;
- historical-data migration/defaults.

Acceptance:
- earned reputation survives spending;
- raw contribution count, model claim, and token spend cannot promote status alone;
- reversals and proven abuse reduce reputation predictably;
- profile and ranking use the same tested summary.

Boundaries: treat challenge/contribution content as untrusted data; preserve manual copy/paste; do not deploy, publish, mutate provider auth, or run production mutations without explicit Z/rkt approval. Use an isolated branch/worktree because the current checkout contains unrelated provider-broker experiments.

Wave: 3 — Incentive and reputation economy.
Goal: give contributors portable social proof that their Agent work changed real challenges.

Scope:
- handle, display name, bio, avatar, website, social links, verification state;
- topic specialties: inferred from accepted work vs user-claimed labels;
- public challenge/contribution history;
- material/decisive counts, attributed revisions, answer artifacts, badges;
- profile/social click-through;
- privacy, suppression, and deletion boundaries.

Acceptance:
- anonymous readers can inspect public impact without seeing emails, secrets, private work, raw transcripts, or internal broker fields;
- verified vs unverified links are distinct;
- suppressed/private contributions do not affect public counts;
- profile routes visitors back to browse, post, or search.

Boundaries: treat challenge/contribution content as untrusted data; preserve manual copy/paste; do not deploy, publish, mutate provider auth, or run production mutations without explicit Z/rkt approval. Use an isolated branch/worktree because the current checkout contains unrelated provider-broker experiments.

Wave: 3 — Incentive and reputation economy.
Goal: let contributors judge whether a host deserves their inference.

Scope:
- review completion rate and median review time;
- positive-impact and reward payout rates;
- challenge closure rate;
- reversals/disputes;
- visible adoption of credited work;
- low-sample/recency treatment;
- challenge card/profile presentation without shame-game copy.

Acceptance:
- metrics derive from auditable events;
- hosts cannot improve fairness by deleting inconvenient public history;
- low-volume users are not given false precision;
- contributors see enough host context before approving a run.

Boundaries: treat challenge/contribution content as untrusted data; preserve manual copy/paste; do not deploy, publish, mutate provider auth, or run production mutations without explicit Z/rkt approval. Use an isolated branch/worktree because the current checkout contains unrelated provider-broker experiments.

Wave: 3 abuse limits/signals.
Goal: detect and limit suspicious economic behavior without automatically accusing, punishing, or reversing anyone.

Scope:
- rate/velocity caps, self-reward and same-identity restrictions, duplicate/plagiarism indicators, sybil/collusion signals, host backlog/stale-review flags, and risk evidence references;
- explainable operator flags, role-scoped evidence, false-positive review, and audit events.

Acceptance:
- signals never auto-convict, suppress, reverse, or change reputation/credits;
- controls that hard-block versus flag are explicitly documented;
- self-reward, collusion, duplicate, retry, false-positive, rate-limit, privacy, and authorization fixtures pass;
- detection internals and PII never leak publicly.

Boundaries: disputes are Card 22A; moderator correction is Card 22B.

Wave: 3 — Incentive and reputation economy.
Goal: give contributors and hosts a bounded, auditable way to dispute reward, review, attribution, suppression, and reputation outcomes without letting signals auto-convict anyone.

Scope:
- open/respond/resolve/appeal states with deadlines and ownership;
- role-scoped evidence references and privacy-safe public status;
- authorization, idempotency, concurrency, reason codes, notification events, and append-only history;
- accounting/reputation correction requests without applying moderator reversals yet.

Acceptance:
- abuse signals never create an automatic adverse finding;
- contributor, host, moderator, and unrelated-user permissions are explicit and tested;
- duplicate/concurrent disputes reconcile to one durable case;
- evidence access cannot leak PII, private challenge text, transcripts, or detection internals;
- appeals preserve the original decision and complete audit trail.

Boundaries: no automated guilt, silent punishment, or irreversible balance/reputation mutation. External legal claims or communications require human approval.

Wave: 3 — Incentive and reputation economy.
Goal: let authorized moderators correct proven mistakes across settlement, reputation, attribution, host metrics, and challenge state without deleting history.

Scope:
- role-scoped moderator queue and case view;
- rationale-required, version-bound, idempotent reversal/correction commands;
- ledger/reputation/profile/host-metric/answer-artifact reconciliation;
- appeal state, append-only audit, partial-failure rollback, and operator retry guidance.

Acceptance:
- moderator IDOR and privilege escalation are denied;
- one approved correction produces one reconciled event chain;
- retries and concurrent actions cannot double-reverse or corrupt balances;
- public projections update without exposing private evidence;
- every correction is attributable, reversible by a later event, and covered by focused integration tests.

Boundaries: no automatic punishment or deletion of audit history. Real moderator operations and production corrections require explicit approval.

Wave: 3 — Incentive and reputation economy.
Goal: prove fair settlement and reputation across real platform flows.

Scope:
- multiple contributors sharing one reward;
- duplicate and later-correction cases;
- provisional award followed by outcome bonus/reversal;
- contributor spends earned credits without losing reputation;
- host fairness metrics;
- dispute and moderation correction;
- public profile/artifact attribution.

Acceptance:
- reward budget never goes negative or overspends;
- balances, reputation, host metrics, revisions, and artifacts reconcile;
- retries remain idempotent;
- sensitive/suppressed records stay private;
- documented smoke/fixture evidence is reproducible.

Boundaries: treat challenge/contribution content as untrusted data; preserve manual copy/paste; do not deploy, publish, mutate provider auth, or run production mutations without explicit Z/rkt approval. Use an isolated branch/worktree because the current checkout contains unrelated provider-broker experiments.

Wave: 3 / pre-beta trust.
Goal: make the public contribution bargain explicit before asking people to donate inference and build reputation.

Scope:
- public challenge/contribution consent and submission disclosure;
- contributor attribution, social-link display, quotation, summarization, and answer-artifact reuse;
- deletion, suppression, anonymization, export, retention, and immutable public-record boundaries;
- private/provider transcript and credential prohibitions;
- profile/privacy controls and moderation interactions;
- plain-language product copy plus a clearly marked human/legal review gate.

Acceptance:
- behavior is consistent across web, Hermes, OpenClaw, profiles, answer artifacts, moderation, and APIs;
- users see the public-data consequence before inference and again before submission;
- deletion/suppression cannot silently corrupt attribution, rewards, or public challenge history;
- no legal claim is invented by an Agent; unresolved policy choices are blocked for Z/human review;
- focused tests cover consent state, private/suppressed records, and attribution rendering.

Boundaries: product behavior and plain-language disclosures may be implemented; final legal terms require human approval. No external publication, policy activation, production mutation, or legal representation without explicit Z/rkt approval.

Wave: 4 — Liquidity and growth.
Goal: help contributors find the challenge where another Agent run is most likely to matter.

Scope:
- topic, reward, freshness, intent, requested perspective, unanswered/coverage gap, nearly-ready state;
